const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Video Abstract Service","serviceType":"Video abstract scripting, animation, voiceover and captioning","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/publication-support/video-abstract/","description":"Video abstracts for published research: script by a subject expert, figures rebuilt for screen, professional voiceover and captions in your style.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a video abstract, and what is it for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A short video summary of a published article — usually one to three minutes, usually animated, always captioned. Most people call it a video abstract; some journals call it a research video or a visual abstract, and they mean the same thing. Journals run them on the article page, in their feeds and in their newsletters, and authors use them at conferences, in seminars, on institutional repositories and in grant reports. Its job is not to replace the paper but to reach the people who were never going to open it, and to give them enough to decide that they should."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long should a video abstract be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"One to three minutes, and shorter is harder. Two and a half minutes is about three hundred and seventy words of voiceover, since a professional read runs near a hundred and fifty words a minute. Check the journal first — many set a maximum and some set a minimum, and a file that misses either is rejected at upload. Beyond three minutes, completion rates fall sharply whatever the subject."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What should a video abstract include?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A hook that states the question, the gap it addresses, enough method to make the result credible, the primary finding with its actual numbers, what it means with its limitation, and an end card carrying the DOI, the journal, funding, competing interests and a contact. What it should not include is a logo sequence at the front, the abstract read aloud in order, or a literature review — those three account for most of the videos nobody finishes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I write the script?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"To a clock, and in a different order from the paper. Work out the runtime, convert it to a word budget at a hundred and fifty words a minute, then decide the single finding the video is about and put it in the first thirty seconds rather than the last. Write for the ear: short sentences, no subordinate clauses, numbers rounded to what can be heard once. Then read it aloud against a stopwatch, because a script that looks right on the page is usually forty seconds long."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I reuse the figures from my paper?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not as screenshots. A journal figure is designed for a page read at arm’s length; on a phone its labels are unreadable and eleven series are five too many. Rebuilding from your source data lets the figure be simplified, scaled for screen and — more importantly — built on in step with the narration, so the viewer watches the point being made rather than meeting a finished chart and searching it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to appear on camera?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and most authors choose not to. Animation with a professional voiceover is easier to caption, easier to revise, easier to produce to a consistent standard and does not depend on your filming conditions. Some journals do ask for a presenter on camera, and a few authors prefer it because it puts a face to the work — where you want that, a short piece to camera can open or close an otherwise animated video."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do video abstracts actually increase citations?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The honest answer is that the evidence is mixed and mostly observational. Papers with video abstracts tend to attract more views, downloads and social attention, but papers that get a video are also the ones authors and editors already think are notable, so the causal claim is hard to separate from the selection. What is not in doubt is reach: a captioned two-minute video is watched by people who do not open PDFs, and that is a reasonable thing to want on its own terms."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What file format and specification does my journal need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually MP4 with H.264 video, at 1080p, 16:9, under a stated file size, with captions supplied as a separate subtitle file as well as burned in. Beyond that publishers differ on runtime, on whether branding is allowed, on licensing, and on whether they host the file or link to a platform. It is all in the journal’s multimedia guidelines, which are a separate document from the instructions for authors and are the one most people never find."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When should I make one — before or after acceptance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"After acceptance, and usually after the proofs. Revisions change numbers, and a number that changes after the voiceover is recorded means a re-record. Starting two to three weeks before the publication date works well: the video is ready for the day the paper appears, which is when the attention exists. A few journals ask for the video at submission — where yours does, allow for a version that may need updating if the revision changes a result."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who owns the video, and can I use it elsewhere?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You do. The finished video, the cuts, the audio and the project files are yours, and you can put them on your institutional repository, a conference screen, your own channels or a grant report. The one thing to check is the journal’s own licence terms, since a few publishers ask for exclusivity on the version they host. Where copyrighted material appears — a figure from another paper, a music track — we clear it or replace it before delivery."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Publication support","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/publication-support/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Video abstract","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/publication-support/video-abstract/"}]}];

